  <section xml:id="assessments">
    <title>Assessments</title>
    <!-- UPDATE by creating a paragraphs for each category of graded work -->
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Written Weekly Homework</title>
      <p>
        Each week you will submit solutions to selected homework problems. Some of the problems will be graded for correctness by a grader assigned to work for me. Others will be graded for completness. Homework will be submitted via Gradescope and is due by 2300 (almost) every Tuesday, starting in the third week of classes. For computing course grades, each homework homework grade will be normalized to be a real number between <m>0</m> and <m>4</m> (possibly slightly higher than <m>4</m>) in a manner specified on Canvas. Your lowest homework score will be dropped, and the other <m>10</m> will be averaged.
      </p>

      <p>
        For graded homework problems, tests, and the final exam, each problem will be assigned an integer score between <m>0</m> and <m>4</m> based on the following rubric:
      </p>

        <tabular>
          <col halign="center"> </col> <col width="80%"> </col>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Grade</cell>
            <cell>Description</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell><p>Good work with minor (generally computational) errors. Explanation complete or has only small gaps. (<m>\sim</m>A)</p></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell><p>Good work with more serious (generally conceptual) errors. Explanation is complete, but may be insufficiently clear. (<m>\sim</m>B)</p></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell><p>Significant, but incomplete, work that could clearly lead to a correct answer is presented, and some attempt at explanation is made. (<m>\sim</m>C)</p></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell><p>Some work that might lead to a complete solution is presented. <alert>Work, even if correct, without explanation cannot receive a higher score.</alert> (<m>\sim</m>D)</p></cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell><p>No progress, no relevant information, or illegible. (<m>\sim</m>F)</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          Occasionally, exceptional work will receive a grade of <m>5</m>, generally based on high-quality explanations and/or very organized computations accompanying the explanations.
          A score of <m>5</m> should be viewed as extra credit for outstanding work and not a routinely-expected score.
        </p>


    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Peer Instruction Participation</title>
<p>
  <q>Peer instruction</q> describes an instructional strategy that involves answering (typically) multiple choice questions using a technological solution, having you discuss your answers with your peers after voting has closed, and then voting again. Although this strategy developed in physics, its efficacy in a number of areas has been demonstrated through educational research. We will frequently use peer instruction in class, and Runestone Academy will be our means of voting. A component of your grade will be based on the fact that you have voted on these questions. Because they are part of the learning experience, correctness of responses will not be part of your grade. You will receive an integer score for peer instruction participation determined by the percentage of all peer instruction questions you answer over the course of the semester.
</p>

<tabular>
  <col halign="center" right="minor"/> 
  <col halign="center"/>
  <col halign="center"/>
  <col halign="center"/>
  <col halign="center"/>
  <col halign="center"/>
  <col halign="center"/>
  <col halign="center"/>
  <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>PI Grade/Prep Grade</cell>
    <cell>5</cell>
    <cell>4</cell>
    <cell>3</cell>
    <cell>2</cell>
    <cell>1</cell>
    <cell>0</cell>
  </row>

  <row>
    <cell>Percent Answered</cell>
    <cell> <m>[90,100]</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>[80,90)</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>[70,80)</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>[60,70)</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>[50,60)</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>[0,50)</m> </cell>
  </row>
</tabular>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Class Preparation Assignments</title>
      <p>
        In order to be able to effectively use our time together in class, before most class meetings you will have a class preparation assignment that will be submitted via Runestone Academy. A typical class preparation assignment will consist of reading assigned portions of the textbook and answering some questions to help me assess what parts of the reading need the most discussion in classtime. Because these assignments are part of the learning experience, correctness of responses will not be part of your grade. You will receive an integer score for your overall class preparation assignment grade. It will be determined by the percentage of all points you earn for class preparation assignments over the course of the semester. The same scale will be used as for peer instruction grades above.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Weekly Exit Tickets</title>
      <p>
        We will spend most of each class period actively working together through group activities to help you develop a deep understanding of the course content. In order to help me keep track of your learning progress throughout the semester, you'll complete a brief exit ticket on Canvas each week. Exit Tickets will be due every Thursday by 2300. Each exit ticket will be worth four points, and your exit ticket grade will be a real number between <m>0</m> and <m>4</m> that is the average of your scores on your exit tickets with the lowest exit ticket dropped.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Tests and Final Exam</title>
      <!-- UPDATE with specifics for your course -->
      <p>
        You will take two non-cumulative tests in class on Thursday, 9 October 2025, and Thursday, 20 November 2025. The final exam will be held as scheduled by the Office of the Registrar on Thursday, 18 December 2025, from 1005 to 1205 CST. The location for the final exam will be announced at a later date. Make-up tests will only be allowed for students who have a substantiated excuse approved by the instructor <em>before the date of the test</em>. For students with another valid reason for a make-up test, in the event that one cannot be arranged within a reasonable time frame, a fair system is in place to replace your grade on the missed test. <alert>Our final exam is near the end of the final examination period. The final exam will not be given before the scheduled time. You are expected to be present for the final exam as scheduled.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        During exams, students will not be allowed to use any notes or electronic devices. Ample space will be provided on the exams for you to show your work. You must bring a writing utensil.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </section>
